<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Synod of Whitby</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/664">664</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Synod of Whitby was a church council held in 664 at the abbey of Whitby, presided over by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiu">Oswiu of Northumbria</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a> argued for Roman practice and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Colmán for the Irish tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a> in calculating the date of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a>; Oswiu chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Colmán withdrew to Iona, and the English church moved closer to Rome.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Seventh-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> knew two ways of fixing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a>. The monks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a> and its daughter houses kept the 84-year Latercus cycle devised by Sulpicius Severus about 410, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by then followed a 19-year cycle adopted from the church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. In Northumbria both had royal patrons: Edwin, converted by missionaries Pope Gregory the Great had sent, established the 19-year cycle, while Oswald, who had learned his faith from the monks of Iona as an exile, brought in Ionan missionaries such as Bishop Aidan. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiu">Oswiu</a>'s court Queen Eanfled kept Easter on a different day from the king, so that one faction feasted while the other still fasted for Lent.</p><p>The difference caused no crisis while Aidan lived, but his successor Finan was challenged by Ronan, an Irishman trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and under Colmán the matter needed royal resolution. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiu">Oswiu</a>'s son Alchfrith, sub-king in Deira, had expelled Ionan monks from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ripon">Ripon</a> in the early 660s and given the monastery to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a>, newly returned from Rome; Henry Mayr-Harting saw him as the chief cause of the trouble that led to the synod.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The synod met at Hilda's double monastery of Streanæshalch. Eanfled sent her chaplain Romanus, and the Frankish bishop Agilbert took the Roman side, but since he could not put the arguments in Old English, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a> spoke for it. Colmán rested his case on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a> and the tradition of the apostle John. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>, Wilfrid answered that the Roman practice was kept in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where Peter and Paul were buried, and by the whole Church as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Oswiu had both sides agree that Christ had given Peter the keys of the kingdom of heaven, and ruled for Peter's practice. Wilfrid's reckoning was in fact the Alexandrian one.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The ruling also settled the form of the monastic tonsure. The episcopal seat of Northumbria moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a> later became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiu">Oswiu</a> replaced the departing clergy mostly with Irishmen from parts of Ireland that kept the Roman Easter, as most of Ireland had done for some time.</p><p>Historians stress that the synod was a Northumbrian affair, one of many councils on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Christendom</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a>'s advocacy has been called a triumphant push against an open door. It was nonetheless a step in the Romanisation of the church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a> it has often been read as the subjugation of a Celtic Church to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, an idea Patrick Wormald called maddeningly ineradicable from the minds of students.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Synod of Whitby” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Picts and to the English: in 635 the Iona monk Aidan founded Lindisfarne at the invitation of King Oswald of Northumbria, who had spent his exile among the Gaels, and bishops trained in the Iona tradition led the Northumbrian church until the <b>Synod of Whitby</b> in 664.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dal-riata">Dal Riata · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 664 Oswiu presided over the <b>Synod of Whitby</b>, which decided that the Northumbrian church would follow Roman rather than Irish practice in calculating Easter, a choice with lasting consequences for the English church.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
